  "llm_analysis": {
    "quality_summary": "The sales_data table contains 186 rows with generally good completeness (no nulls in most columns, only 2.69% nulls in store_id). Data types are appropriate, but there are several quality concerns: mixed date formats in transaction_date, negative values in quantity and total_amount indicating returns, inconsistent category naming (e.g., \"Sport\" vs \"Sports\"), and irregular values in customer_segment (e.g., \"Prem\", \"Bgt\"). The products and stores tables are small and clean with no nulls and consistent formats.",
    "critical_issues": [
      "Negative quantity values (e.g., -1, -2) representing returns are not flagged, causing potential misinterpretation of sales volume.",
      "total_amount contains negative values that should be aligned with return transactions.",
      "transaction_date mixes formats (MM/DD/YYYY and YYYY-MM-DD), hindering date parsing.",
      "store_id has 2.69% nulls, which will break joins with the stores table.",
      "customer_segment includes inconsistent abbreviations (\"Prem\", \"Bgt\") leading to fragmented segmentation.",
      "category values are not standardized (\"Sport\" vs \"Sports\")."
    ],
    "transformation_recommendations": {
      "sales_data.quantity": "Create a flag column (e.g., is_return) for negative quantities and convert quantity to absolute value for net sales calculations.",
      "sales_data.total_amount": "Align total_amount sign with the is_return flag; consider storing return amounts as positive values in a separate column.",
      "sales_data.transaction_date": "Standardize all dates to ISO format (YYYY-MM-DD) using a parsing routine that handles both MM/DD/YYYY and YYYY-MM-DD.",
      "sales_data.store_id": "Impute missing store_id values where possible (e.g., based on transaction patterns) or flag rows for review before joining.",
      "sales_data.customer_segment": "Map all segment abbreviations to a canonical set (e.g., \"Premium\", \"Budget\", \"Loyal\", \"Standard\").",
      "sales_data.category": "Standardize category names to match the taxonomy used in the products table (e.g., convert \"Sport\" to \"Sports\").",
      "sales_data.transaction_id": "Validate that IDs follow expected prefixes (e.g., TXN for sales, RET for returns) and flag anomalies.",
      "sales_data.unit_price": "Verify that unit_price matches the standard_price from the products table; flag discrepancies for pricing audits."
    },
    "join_key_reasoning": "The candidate join keys store_id and product_id are the most logical connectors because they appear in both sales_data and the dimension tables (stores, products) with matching data types. store_id enables linking each transaction to its physical location and region attributes, while product_id allows enriching sales records with product details such as category and standard_price. Ensuring these keys are clean (no nulls, consistent formatting) is essential for accurate dimensional joins."
  }
